    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "A council wants to know how residents travel to work. It interviews $180$ people at a bus stop on Tuesday morning. Explain why this sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "Only people at this bus stop at that time can be included.\nResidents who drive, walk, cycle or work from home are missed.\nBus users are over-represented, so the sample may not reflect how all residents travel to work."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A council wants residents' opinions about a new road. It asks $4$ residents from a community of $720$. Explain why this sample may be unrepresentative.",
        "solution_text": "Four residents may miss much of the variety of opinions in the community.\nThe sample is too small to provide a strong basis for describing the whole community's views."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A website receives $650$ voluntary responses to a survey about whether people enjoy completing surveys. Almost all respondents say yes. Explain why it cannot conclude that almost everyone enjoys surveys.",
        "solution_text": "The sample includes only people who choose to complete the survey.\nPeople who dislike surveys may be less likely to respond.\nThe respondents may not represent the whole population, so their answers do not justify the conclusion."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A researcher investigates whether new businesses usually succeed by interviewing the owners of $75$ businesses that are still operating. Explain why the sample may be unrepresentative, with reference to survivorship bias.",
        "solution_text": "The sample includes businesses that survived and are still operating.\nBusinesses that closed are left out.\nThis is survivorship bias: excluding failed businesses may make success appear more common than it is among all new businesses."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Engineers inspect $120$ aircraft that returned from missions. They observe many hits on the wings and body but very few around the cockpit. A statistician suggests placing extra armour around the cockpit. Explain why, with reference to survivorship bias.",
        "solution_text": "The sample includes only aircraft that survived and returned.\nAircraft that did not return are left out, so this is survivorship bias.\nHits around the cockpit may have prevented aircraft from returning.\nFew observed hits around the cockpit do not show that this area is safe.\nExtra armour there may protect against damage that prevents survival, while the observed wing and body damage was survived by the returning aircraft."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "A council wants to investigate internet access among all residents. It randomly selects $210$ residents from a list containing only residents with email addresses. Explain why random selection does not make this sample representative.",
        "solution_text": "Residents without email addresses are missing from the list and cannot be selected.\nTheir internet access may differ from that of residents with email addresses.\nRandom selection from this incomplete list does not remove the exclusion, so the sample may not represent all residents."
      }
    ],
    "methods": [
